    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "This introduction contrasts the historical focus on negative body image with the concept of positive body image (body appreciation) and cites Tylka & Wood-Barcalow (2015a) for a definition of positive body image. The authors state that the current study focused on associations between body appreciation and putative correlates related to the desire to enhance or express one's appearance.",
      "evidence_source": "\"Recent research has indicated that positive body image is in fact a separate construct from negative body image, with its own unique dimensions and correlates (Tylka &amp; Wood-Barcalow, 2015a).\" \"Tylka and Wood-Barcalow (2015a) define positive body image as body appreciation, loving and feeling comfort with one's body, having broad ideas of attractiveness, adaptive self-care for appearance, manifesting positive feelings through the body, and filtering information about the body in protective ways.\" \"The current study focused on associations between body appreciation and putative correlates that relate to the desire to enhance or to express one's appearance.\"",
